WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.560
In 2017, the Consumer Financial
Protection Bureau sued the Student Loan

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:13.500
Servicing company Navient, alleging it
steered borrowers away from generous

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.090
income-based repayment plans. Media
coverage of the suit immediately and

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:22.350
overwhelmingly sided with the CFPB. But
recent evidence in the case shows why

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:27.300
some healthy skepticism may be in order.
The alleged victims, it turns out, were

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.070
informed of income-based repayment — some
as many as thirty times. They either didn't

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:36.570
want it — one called it "stupid as f***" to a
Navient representative — or didn't follow

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:41.670
through on paperwork, or didn't qualify.
CFPB's witnesses were also caught lying

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:45.719
to Navient. Or failing to pay their
government loans while making payments

00:00:45.719 --> 00:00:50.219
on luxury automobiles. Others refused to
provide income documents to Navient, or

00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:53.730
were wealthy, which is why they weren't
eligible for the benefits they were

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:58.109
allegedly denied. How was a case like
this allowed to proceed  and drag on for

00:00:58.109 --> 00:01:03.960
years? Because in this case, lawmakers, the
courts, and a credulous media have given

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.020
too much deference to the CFPB.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.360
Who do you think has the stronger case: Consumer

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.860
Financial Protection Bureau, or Navient?
Let us know in our poll. Also, let us know

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.430
what other topics you'd like our
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:22.250
sure to like and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

